12_partial_dump — periodic partial-result dump integration case (issue #193)

Runs 200 primaries (150 MeV protons in water, NUCRE off, fixed RNDSEED) with a
deterministic count cadence, `--dump-every-primaries 40` (see args.cmake). The
run reaches five family-complete checkpoints and, at the first four, overwrites
NB_msh.dat with the exact partial result.

Because a dump is a non-destructive snapshot (an out-of-place postprocess of a
shadow copy, never the live accumulators), the FINAL saved output must be
identical — within compare_dat.py's tolerance, well inside floating-point
reduction-order noise — to a plain no-dump run of the same 200 primaries. That
reference is expected/NB_msh.dat, captured from `openshieldhit -n 200` without any
dump flags. compare_dat.py ignores `#` comment lines, so the extra
`# COMPLETENESS: exact` header the dump path stamps does not affect the match.

What this case exercises end to end: `--dump-every-primaries` flag parsing, the
LIVE checkpoint batching it implies, the file sink that writes each dump, the
completeness stamp, and the guarantee that periodic dumps do not perturb the run.
The deterministic "dump at K primaries == a truncated K-primary run" invariant it
rests on is proven at the unit level in
tests/unit/test_osh_simulation.c::test_dump_control_is_non_destructive and the
checkpoint-batching invariance tests.
